  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 229 PM MDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nStarting Sunday, our region is finally getting relief from the \nstretch of unseasonably warm temperatures. High temperatures are\nforecast in the 60s and 70s for Sunday. We are forecast to be \nin a southwest upper-level flow with an incoming trough west of \nthe Rocky Mountains. This trough will traverse east across the \ncounty warning area (CWA) bringing chances for precipitation \nbeginning Sunday afternoon and continuing overnight into Monday.\nThe entire CWA has chances for precipitation, but the western \nportion has the greatest with Probability of Precipitation \n(PoPs) around 60-75%. Storms are forecast to enter the area from\nthe west and traverse east across the CWA. The environment is \nmarginally severe in terms of CAPE, lapse rates and shear, so \nhazard wise we are likely looking at rain showers. A stronger \nstorm could latch onto the instability in environment and \nproduce strong winds around 60 mph and hail around 1 inch. \nStrong winds gusts are also possible with any short-lived or \ncollapsing storms that are pop up and dissipate quickly. Weak \nshowers will likely persist into early Monday morning.\n\nMonday through the end of the week is looking fairly consistent\nforecast wise. High temperatures are forecast in the 60s to 70s\nwith afternoon PoPs around 30-50% due to embedded shortwave and\njet support. Starting Monday, we transition to more of a zonal \nupper- level pattern with a jet streak overhead. Early \nWednesday, we are forecast to transition to a weak ridging \npattern with a jet streak remaining overhead. The jet streak \noverhead the CWA will cause windy conditions Tuesday afternoon \nthrough Wednesday with gusts from 30-40 mph possible for the \nwestern CWA. Increased jet support is expected to remain over \nthe area through at least Thursday, but models diverge on when \nthe jet streak will move on. This will ultimately affect \nprecipitation chances for the CWA.",
